Finding: {"title":"Local-timezone 'today' compared against UTC-anchored date strings in schedule status logic","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"Training plan course/payment schedule status","files":["src/lib/training-plan-course-schedule.ts","src/lib/training-plan-payment-schedule-view.ts"],"detail":"getCourseScheduleStatus (training-plan-course-schedule.ts, line 108) and getStatus (training-plan-payment-schedule-view.ts, line 108) both compute 'today' via date.getFullYear()/getMonth()/getDate() — local server timezone getters — then string-compare against startDate/endDate/dueDate values that are date-only strings elsewhere parsed as UTC midnight (parseDateOnly in the same course-schedule file explicitly uses T00:00:00.000Z). If the server's local timezone is behind UTC (e.g. US timezones), local 'today' can differ from the UTC calendar date by hours around midnight.","impact":"A course or payment installment can flip between upcoming/scheduled/due/overdue/past_target up to several hours early or late relative to the intended UTC-anchored day boundary. This is a duplicated bug pattern reproduced independently in two files rather than one isolated slip.","recommendation":"Compute 'today' the same way parseDateOnly treats stored dates — via UTC getters or toISOString().slice(0,10) — and factor the logic into one shared helper both files import.","confidence":"medium"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

